export const DATA_46: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-054", num: 54, title: "Đề 054", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "A Day at the National Park", paragraphs: ["Every year, millions of families, students, and nature lovers visit national parks around the world to enjoy the beauty of the natural environment. Whether it is a vast forest, a rocky mountain range, a quiet lake, or a wide open plain, national parks offer people a chance to step away from the busy rhythms of city life and experience nature up close. For many visitors, a day spent at a national park is not just a day off — it is a chance to learn, relax, and reconnect with the world beyond screens and schedules.", "Planning ahead is one of the most important steps before visiting a national park. Most parks ask visitors to buy tickets or make reservations in advance, especially during busy seasons such as summer holidays or public festivals. Arriving without a booking can mean long waits at the entrance or, in some popular parks, being turned away entirely. It is also a good idea to check the park's official website for information about opening hours, trail conditions, and any areas that may be temporarily closed for wildlife protection or environmental repair. Visitors who plan carefully tend to have a much smoother and more enjoyable experience than those who arrive without preparation.", "Once inside the park, visitors can choose from a wide variety of activities depending on their interests and fitness levels. Hiking is one of the most popular choices, with trails ranging from gentle walks suitable for young children and older adults to challenging climbs that take experienced hikers several hours to complete. Many parks also offer guided ranger tours, bird-watching programmes, photography workshops, and educational talks about the local ecosystem. Families with young children often find that junior ranger programmes — in which children complete activity booklets and earn a special badge — are a rewarding way to keep younger visitors engaged and learning throughout the day.", "While enjoying the park, visitors are expected to follow a set of basic rules designed to protect both the environment and other guests. The most fundamental of these is the \"leave no trace\" principle, which asks visitors to take all of their rubbish home with them rather than leaving it in the park. Feeding wild animals is strictly prohibited , as human food can damage the health of wildlife and make animals dangerously accustomed to approaching people. Visitors must also stay on designated trails to avoid trampling fragile plant life or disturbing nesting birds. These rules may seem straightforward, but rangers report that violations — particularly littering and wildlife feeding — remain among the most common problems they deal with on a daily basis.", "Safety is another important consideration. Weather in national parks can change rapidly, and visitors who set out on a long hike under clear skies may find themselves caught in sudden rain, strong winds, or even snowfall, depending on the season and altitude. It is strongly recommended that hikers carry enough water, high-energy snacks, a basic first aid kit, and a fully charged mobile phone or a paper map, since mobile signals are often unreliable in remote park areas. Children should always remain within sight of an adult, and visitors with health conditions should check with a doctor before attempting strenuous activities.", "After a full day of exploration, many visitors finish their park experience at the visitor centre, where they can browse exhibitions about the local geography, geology, and wildlife, buy souvenirs, and speak with rangers about what they have seen. Some parks also have picnic areas, cafes, or on-site accommodation for those who wish to extend their stay into the evening or overnight. For many people, the highlight of the day is not any particular activity but simply the feeling of being surrounded by nature — breathing clean air, hearing birdsong rather than traffic, and watching the sky change colour as the sun moves across it.", "National parks exist because societies have made a deliberate choice to protect certain natural spaces from development and commercial use, preserving them for both current and future generations. Visiting one is therefore both a pleasure and a small act of appreciation — a reminder that these places exist because people before us decided they were worth protecting, and that keeping them healthy depends on the care and respect of everyone who walks through their gates."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Visiting a national park offers enjoyment and learning but requires preparation, responsible behaviour, and awareness of safety.", "The most important reason to visit a national park is to participate in guided ranger tours and photography workshops.", "National parks are too expensive and difficult to access for most ordinary families.", "National parks should replace city parks because they offer a far wider range of activities for visitors of all ages."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what may happen if visitors arrive at a popular national park without a booking?", options: ["They may face long queues or be refused entry to the park altogether.", "They are usually directed to a less crowded alternative park nearby.", "They will be required to pay a higher entrance fee than those who reserved in advance.", "They will only be permitted to visit the visitor centre and not the trails."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"rewarding\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["giving a sense of satisfaction or meaningful benefit", "suitable only for children with academic interests", "physically tiring but necessary for building discipline", "expensive but worth the investment for families"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one purpose of junior ranger programmes for young visitors?", options: ["They allow children to take part in challenging hikes alongside professional rangers.", "They give children a way to stay engaged and continue learning throughout their visit.", "They prepare children to become qualified wildlife rangers when they grow up.", "They replace the need for parents to supervise young children inside the park."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["visitors who arrive without making any preparation", "families travelling with young children", "park rangers on duty at the entrance", "tourists visiting during public festivals"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is feeding wild animals strictly forbidden in national parks?", options: ["It distracts animals from their natural feeding patterns and reduces biodiversity.", "It causes animals to abandon their natural habitats and move closer to urban areas.", "Human food harms the health of wildlife and causes animals to lose their fear of people.", "Feeding animals attracts large crowds that disturb other visitors trying to enjoy the park."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"prohibited\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["recommended only for experienced wildlife handlers", "not allowed under any circumstances", "strongly encouraged as part of responsible tourism", "permitted only with a special ranger licence"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following items is recommended for hikers to carry for safety?", options: ["An emergency tent in case they need to spend an unexpected night outdoors.", "A basic first aid kit along with enough water and high-energy snacks.", "A printed copy of the park's rules and regulations to follow during the hike.", "A professional camera to document changes in weather conditions."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT listed as a recommended item for hikers to bring?", options: ["High-energy snacks to maintain energy levels.", "A fully charged mobile phone or a paper map.", "Sufficient water for the duration of the hike.", "A detailed guidebook identifying local plant and animal species."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraph 4 about visitors' behaviour in national parks?", options: ["Rangers spend most of their time giving educational talks rather than enforcing park regulations.", "Most visitors follow the rules carefully because they are passionate about environmental conservation.", "Despite clear rules being in place, many visitors still litter and feed wildlife regularly.", "The \"leave no trace\" principle is so well known that violations have become extremely rare in recent years."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Importance of Critical Thinking", paragraphs: ["In an age defined by information overload, social media echo chambers, and the rapid spread of misinformation, the ability to think critically has never been more valuable. Critical thinking — broadly defined as the capacity to analyse information objectively, evaluate evidence carefully, and reach reasoned conclusions rather than accepting claims at face value — is increasingly recognised by educators, employers, and policy makers as one of the most essential skills a person can develop. Yet despite its acknowledged importance, critical thinking is rarely taught systematically in schools, and many adults navigate a complex information environment with few reliable tools for distinguishing credible sources from misleading ones.", "At its core, critical thinking involves a set of interrelated mental habits rather than a single, fixed technique. Skilled critical thinkers ask questions before accepting information: Who produced this content, and what might their motivations be? What evidence is offered in support of this claim, and how strong is that evidence? Are there alternative explanations that the source has not considered or has chosen to ignore? Is the conclusion drawn actually supported by the evidence presented, or does it require an unjustified logical leap? Practising these habits consistently — even when evaluating information that confirms one's existing beliefs — is what separates genuine critical thinkers from those who apply scepticism selectively and only to views they already dislike.", "One of the most significant obstacles to critical thinking is a cluster of cognitive tendencies that psychologists call cognitive biases . Confirmation bias, perhaps the most widely studied of these, describes the tendency to seek out, favour, and more readily remember information that supports what we already believe, while unconsciously dismissing or minimising evidence that contradicts our existing views. Anchoring bias leads people to rely too heavily on the first piece of information they encounter when making a judgement, even when later information is more accurate or relevant. The availability heuristic causes people to overestimate the likelihood of events that come easily to mind — such as dramatic news stories — while underestimating risks that receive less media coverage. Understanding that these biases exist and operate unconsciously is a necessary first step toward counteracting them .", "The relationship between critical thinking and education is complex. Research suggests that while intelligence and academic knowledge provide a useful foundation, they do not by themselves produce critical thinkers. A student who has memorised large amounts of factual information but has never been asked to question assumptions, evaluate competing claims, or construct and defend an argument may perform well on traditional examinations while remaining highly susceptible to manipulation in real-world contexts. Educational systems that prioritise rote learning over open inquiry therefore risk producing graduates who are knowledgeable but not genuinely analytical. Conversely, studies have found that students who are regularly exposed to Socratic questioning, debate, and case-based problem solving develop measurably stronger critical thinking capacities over time.", "In professional contexts, the demand for critical thinking has grown substantially as automation and artificial intelligence have taken over many of the routine analytical tasks once performed by human workers. Employers increasingly report that what they value most in new hires is not the possession of specific technical knowledge — which can often be acquired on the job — but the ability to approach novel problems with sound reasoning, evaluate the reliability of data sources, and communicate well-supported conclusions to diverse audiences. In fields as varied as medicine, law, journalism, and business management, the capacity to think clearly under conditions of uncertainty and incomplete information is what distinguishes competent practitioners from truly excellent ones. [1]", "Social media has introduced a particularly acute challenge to critical thinking at a cultural level. [2] Platforms are algorithmically designed to maximise engagement, which in practice means that emotionally charged, polarising, or sensational content tends to receive more visibility than nuanced, carefully reasoned material. [3] The speed at which content circulates online also works against the kind of deliberate, evidence-based evaluation that critical thinking requires: users encounter dozens of claims per minute and are culturally incentivised to respond quickly — sharing, liking, or commenting — before they have had time to verify the information they have encountered. [4] Several studies have found that false information spreads significantly faster on social media platforms than accurate information, a phenomenon attributed partly to the novelty and emotional intensity of misinformation.", "Cultivating critical thinking is not a passive process — it requires deliberate, sustained effort and a genuine willingness to revise one's beliefs in the face of contradictory evidence. This can be psychologically uncomfortable, as it demands a degree of intellectual humility: the recognition that one's current understanding may be incomplete, biased, or simply wrong. But it is precisely this discomfort that makes critical thinking so valuable. In a world where confident misinformation spreads faster than careful analysis, the capacity to slow down, question assumptions, and follow evidence wherever it leads remains one of the most powerful — and most urgently needed — intellectual tools available to any individual or society."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["An argument that critical thinking is an essential skill increasingly under threat from cognitive biases, poor education, and social media.", "A guide to identifying and avoiding the most common types of online misinformation.", "A comparison of critical thinking abilities across different professions and academic disciplines.", "An explanation of why universities should replace traditional examinations with debate and Socratic questioning."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one question that skilled critical thinkers ask when evaluating information?", options: ["How many other people have read and agreed with this piece of information?", "Who produced this content and what motivations might they have had?", "Does this information appear in more than three different sources?", "Has this information been approved by a recognised academic institution?"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"interrelated\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["simple enough to be practised without any prior instruction", "connected to and dependent on one another", "organised in a strict sequence from basic to advanced", "highly specialised and requiring formal academic training"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does confirmation bias affect the way people process information?", options: ["It encourages people to always rely on the first piece of information they encounter on a topic.", "It makes people excessively focused on dramatic news stories while ignoring everyday events.", "It causes people to favour information that supports their existing beliefs while dismissing contradictory evidence.", "It leads people to spend too much time checking facts before forming any opinion."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 (\"a necessary first step toward counteracting them \") refers to _______.", options: ["psychologists who study human decision-making", "cognitive biases such as confirmation bias and anchoring bias", "dramatic news stories that distort risk perception", "people who apply scepticism selectively"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what type of educational experience has been found to strengthen critical thinking in students?", options: ["Focusing exclusively on mathematics and logic-based subjects from an early age.", "Memorising large amounts of factual content across a wide range of academic subjects.", "Encouraging students to study independently without teacher-directed instruction.", "Regular exposure to Socratic questioning, structured debate, and case-based problem solving."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"susceptible\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["easily influenced or harmed by something negative", "intellectually curious and open to new ideas", "resistant to outside opinions and criticism", "highly confident in one's own judgements and decisions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have studies found about the spread of false information on social media platforms?", options: ["False information is eventually corrected by platform algorithms within a few hours of being posted.", "Social media platforms have become more effective at slowing the spread of misinformation in recent years.", "Users who share false information are typically aware that the content is inaccurate.", "False information spreads considerably faster than accurate information due partly to its novelty and emotional intensity."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This pressure is felt especially acutely in high-stakes decisions, where the cost of flawed reasoning can be severe and difficult to reverse.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a question that critical thinkers should ask when evaluating a claim?", options: ["How many people have already shared or endorsed this piece of information?", "Is the conclusion actually supported by the evidence, or does it require an unjustified logical leap?", "What evidence is offered in support of the claim, and how reliable is it?", "Are there alternative explanations that the source has not considered?"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Era of Artificial Intelligence: Opportunities and Risks", paragraphs: ["Artificial intelligence has moved from the margins of academic research to the centre of global economic and social life with a speed that has surprised even many of its pioneers. Within a single decade, systems capable of recognising speech, translating languages, diagnosing diseases, and generating written text have progressed from laboratory demonstrations to tools embedded in the daily routines of billions of people. [1] This transformation has been accompanied by an intensity of public debate that reflects a genuine uncertainty about whether the long-term consequences of AI development will ultimately prove more beneficial or more disruptive to human societies.", "The opportunities presented by artificial intelligence are substantial and span an impressive range of domains. In medicine, AI-powered diagnostic tools have demonstrated the ability to detect certain cancers, eye diseases, and cardiac abnormalities from medical imaging with accuracy that matches or, in some cases, surpasses that of experienced clinical specialists. In agriculture, machine learning models are being used to predict crop yields, identify plant diseases at early stages, and optimise irrigation schedules in ways that could make food production significantly more efficient and less wasteful. In education, adaptive learning platforms adjust the difficulty and content of lessons in real time based on a student's performance, offering a degree of personalisation that traditional classroom instruction cannot easily replicate. These applications represent genuine advances that could improve quality of life and reduce inequality, particularly in regions where access to skilled professionals has historically been limited.", "The risks associated with AI are, however, equally broad in scope and more difficult to circumscribe . Labour market disruption is among the most frequently discussed concerns. Economists disagree about the net employment effects of AI-driven automation: some argue that, as with previous technological revolutions, new categories of work will emerge to replace those displaced, while others contend that the speed and breadth of current automation may outpace the economy's capacity for job creation, producing structural unemployment at a scale not previously encountered. What is broadly accepted is that the transition will not be costless, and that workers in routine cognitive and manual occupations face the most immediate exposure to displacement.", "Beyond employment, concerns about algorithmic bias and fairness have attracted sustained attention from researchers and regulators alike. AI systems are trained on historical data, and when that data reflects existing social inequalities — as it often does — the models can perpetuate or even amplify those inequalities in their outputs. Cases have been documented in which facial recognition systems performed with significantly lower accuracy on darker-skinned individuals, and in which loan approval algorithms produced outcomes that disadvantaged applicants from minority communities. These findings highlight the risk that AI, if deployed without adequate oversight, may embed and systematise discrimination in ways that make it both less visible and more difficult to contest than traditional human bias.", "The concentration of AI capabilities within a small number of large technology companies and a handful of nation-states raises further concerns about power and governance. The data, computational resources, and specialised talent required to develop frontier AI systems are distributed with extraordinary unevenness across the global economy. [2] This concentration creates asymmetries of capability that may widen existing geopolitical divides and reduce the capacity of smaller nations and lower-income populations to shape the trajectory of a technology that will profoundly affect their lives. International efforts to establish shared governance frameworks for AI development have made modest progress, but the absence of binding multilateral agreements remains a significant gap in the global regulatory landscape.", "Proponents of accelerated AI development frequently invoke the competitive logic of technological leadership — arguing that nations or institutions that fall behind in AI capability risk not only economic disadvantage but also strategic vulnerability. Critics respond that this framing creates a collective action problem in which the perceived need to move fast discourages the adoption of precautionary measures that might slow development but reduce the probability of serious harm. [3] The challenge for policymakers is therefore not simply one of deciding how much to regulate AI, but of designing regulatory frameworks sophisticated enough to distinguish between applications that genuinely warrant caution and those that can be safely accelerated.", "What is perhaps most striking about the present moment is the degree to which decisions of enormous long-term consequence are being made under conditions of deep uncertainty. [4] The parameters of beneficial and harmful AI remain contested, the mechanisms of oversight are still being developed, and the distributional effects of the technology's deployment are only partially understood. Navigating this uncertainty responsibly will require not only technical expertise but a sustained commitment to inclusive deliberation — ensuring that the communities most affected by AI have a meaningful voice in determining the conditions under which it enters their lives."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best states the central concern of the passage?", options: ["Artificial intelligence is developing too slowly to address urgent global challenges in medicine and education", "The concentration of AI development among wealthy nations makes the technology irrelevant to developing countries", "Governments have already established adequate regulatory frameworks to manage the risks associated with AI deployment", "AI presents both significant opportunities across multiple sectors and substantial risks that remain difficult to govern, requiring careful and inclusive decision-making"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which field does the passage say AI tools have shown accuracy that sometimes exceeds that of experienced professionals?", options: ["Agricultural yield prediction and irrigation optimisation", "Translation of spoken language across multiple dialects", "Personalised lesson planning in primary education", "Detection of certain diseases from medical imaging"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what point of disagreement exists among economists regarding AI-driven automation?", options: ["Whether governments should fund retraining programmes for displaced workers", "Whether AI automation will affect manual workers more severely than cognitive workers", "Whether automation poses a greater risk to developed economies than to developing ones", "Whether the net employment effects will be offset by new job creation or result in structural unemployment"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do the examples of facial recognition systems and loan approval algorithms illustrate?", options: ["The difficulty of obtaining sufficiently large datasets to train accurate AI models", "The risk that AI systems trained on biased historical data can reproduce or worsen existing social inequalities", "The commercial success of AI tools developed by large technology companies", "The tendency of AI systems to favour high-income users over those from lower socioeconomic backgrounds"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 3 and 5, which of the following most accurately describes two distinct risks of AI identified in the passage?", options: ["The risk of structural unemployment among displaced workers and the risk that power concentration widens geopolitical divides", "The risk of students becoming over-reliant on adaptive learning platforms and the risk of medical misdiagnosis by AI systems", "The risk of AI-generated misinformation and the risk of insufficient investment in AI research in lower-income nations", "The risk of energy overconsumption by data centres and the risk of AI replacing human creativity"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT identified as a risk or concern associated with artificial intelligence?", options: ["Displacement of workers in routine cognitive and manual occupations", "Amplification of social inequalities through biased training data", "Uneven global distribution of AI capabilities favouring powerful nations", "The possibility of AI systems developing independent consciousness and acting against human interests"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 4 in the phrase \"more difficult to contest than traditional human bias\" refers to _______.", options: ["The historical data used to train AI systems in commercial applications", "The process by which loan approval algorithms evaluate applicant profiles", "The accuracy gap observed in facial recognition systems across different demographic groups", "The discrimination that AI may embed and systematise in its outputs"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"circumscribe\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To distribute resources evenly among competing stakeholders", "To accelerate the spread of something across different industries", "To measure the precise financial cost of a specific outcome", "To define or set clear limits around the extent of something"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"perpetuate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["To correct imbalances introduced by flawed training methods", "To cause something harmful to continue existing over time", "To accelerate a process beyond its originally intended scope", "To expose hidden patterns within a large dataset"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'In practice, the pace at which AI capabilities are being commercialised has consistently outrun the capacity of legislative bodies to draft, debate, and enact meaningful oversight.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Great Pyramids of Giza: Architectural Wonders of the Ancient World", paragraphs: ["Among the surviving monuments of the ancient world, the pyramids of Giza occupy a category of their own — not merely as feats of engineering, but as enduring provocations to historical understanding. [1] Constructed during the Fourth Dynasty of the Old Kingdom of ancient Egypt, approximately between 2580 and 2510 BCE, the three principal pyramids at Giza were built as funerary monuments for the pharaohs Khufu, Khafre, and Menkaure. That structures of such precision and scale could have been conceived, planned, and executed by a pre-industrial civilisation using tools of copper and stone continues to generate scholarly debate and popular fascination in roughly equal measure.", "The Great Pyramid of Khufu — the largest of the three and the only structure among the original Seven Wonders of the Ancient World still standing — originally rose to a height of approximately 146.5 metres, a record it held as the world's tallest man-made structure for nearly four thousand years. It is estimated to consist of around 2.3 million stone blocks, with individual blocks weighing between 2.5 and 80 tonnes. The precision with which these blocks were cut and positioned is remarkable even by contemporary standards: the base of the pyramid is level to within just 2.1 centimetres across its entire 230-metre perimeter, and its four sides are aligned with the cardinal points of the compass to an accuracy of less than one-tenth of a degree. Such tolerances suggest a degree of surveying and planning sophistication that researchers have found difficult to fully account for within the known technological repertoire of the period.", "The question of how the pyramids were built has occupied scholars for centuries and remains, in several important respects, unresolved. The logistical challenge alone was staggering: delivering, shaping, and positioning millions of stone blocks — many quarried from sites hundreds of kilometres away — within the timeframe implied by the historical record required a workforce of extraordinary scale and organisation. Early modern estimates of a workforce numbering in the hundreds of thousands have been substantially revised downward in light of archaeological evidence. Excavations near Giza conducted since the 1990s have uncovered the remains of a workers' settlement, including bakeries, breweries, and medical facilities, suggesting a permanent, organised labour force of perhaps 20,000 to 30,000 workers — far fewer than earlier accounts supposed, but indicative of a remarkably sophisticated administrative and logistical operation.", "Among the most compelling recent contributions to understanding pyramid construction is the discovery, announced in 2017, of a previously unknown internal corridor within the Great Pyramid, revealed through a technique called muon tomography — a non-invasive scanning method that uses subatomic particles to map dense structures. [2] This discovery, part of an international research initiative called ScanPyramids, demonstrated that significant structural features of the monument remain uncharted, and reopened debate about the internal architecture of the pyramid and the purpose of its known chambers. Whether the newly identified void serves a structural function, represents an unfinished construction element, or conceals further chambers has not yet been determined.", "The Sphinx, located adjacent to the pyramid of Khafre and carved from the bedrock of the Giza plateau, adds a further layer of complexity to the site's interpretive history. Conventionally attributed to the reign of Khafre on the basis of proximity and stylistic analysis, the monument has been the subject of renewed scrutiny following geological studies that some researchers interpret as suggesting a significantly earlier date of construction. [3] The hypothesis, advanced by a minority of scholars, that the erosion patterns on the Sphinx's enclosure walls are consistent with prolonged water weathering — implying construction during a wetter climatic period predating the Fourth Dynasty — has not achieved mainstream acceptance but has proved resistant to definitive refutation, sustaining a controversy that periodically resurfaces in both academic and popular literature.", "The cultural and symbolic dimensions of the Giza complex extend well beyond the architectural. The pyramids functioned not simply as tombs but as components of an elaborate funerary landscape that included causeway temples, mortuary temples, and subsidiary pyramids for royal family members, all oriented and interconnected according to religious and cosmological principles that governed ancient Egyptian conceptions of death, resurrection, and divine kingship. [4] The alignment of the pyramids with celestial bodies — including the stars of Orion's Belt, which some researchers have argued mirrors the spatial arrangement of the three main pyramids — remains a subject of ongoing investigation, though the precise intentionality of such correspondences is difficult to establish from the available evidence.", "For contemporary researchers, the pyramids of Giza represent not a closed chapter of human history but an active field of inquiry in which each new discovery tends to generate as many questions as it resolves. The combination of advanced remote-sensing technologies, archaeometric dating methods, and renewed interest in ancient administrative and logistical systems has transformed what was once assumed to be a well-understood monument into a site of continuing revelation. Whether future investigation will finally settle the most persistent questions about how, and by whom, these structures were built remains uncertain — but the pyramids' capacity to resist complete explanation is itself, perhaps, the most enduring testament to the civilisation that created them."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best describes the overall scope of the passage?", options: ["A technical comparison of different engineering methods used to build monuments across the ancient world", "An argument that the pyramids of Giza were constructed by a civilisation more technologically advanced than Egyptologists currently acknowledge", "A chronological history of ancient Egyptian pharaohs and the religious beliefs that motivated pyramid construction", "An examination of the architectural precision, construction debates, recent discoveries, and cultural significance of the Giza pyramids"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following measurements is given as evidence of the Great Pyramid's extraordinary precision?", options: ["The pyramid held the record as the world's tallest structure for nearly four thousand years", "The base of the pyramid is level to within 2.1 centimetres across its entire perimeter", "The pyramid contains an estimated 2.3 million individual stone blocks", "The pyramid's original height of approximately 146.5 metres"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did excavations near Giza conducted since the 1990s reveal about the pyramid workforce?", options: ["Workers were rotated on a seasonal basis and had no permanent facilities near the construction site", "Evidence of a permanent, organised settlement suggested a workforce of roughly 20,000 to 30,000 workers", "The workforce consisted primarily of enslaved people brought from conquered territories", "The workforce numbered in the hundreds of thousands, confirming earlier historical accounts"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was significant about the 2017 discovery inside the Great Pyramid?", options: ["It proved that the pyramid's internal chambers were deliberately left unfinished by the original builders", "It confirmed that the pyramid contained a sealed burial chamber that had never been opened", "It identified the precise location of Khufu's burial goods using non-invasive scanning equipment", "It used muon tomography to reveal a previously unknown internal corridor, showing that major structural features remain uncharted"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why has the minority hypothesis about the Sphinx's age not been dismissed outright by the academic community?", options: ["Recent excavations beneath the Sphinx have uncovered artefacts inconsistent with a Fourth Dynasty construction date", "Geological dating techniques have confirmed that the Sphinx's enclosure walls predate the Fourth Dynasty", "Although it has not achieved mainstream acceptance, the hypothesis has proved difficult to definitively refute", "It has been endorsed by the majority of Egyptologists specialising in Old Kingdom archaeology"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a component of the funerary landscape surrounding the pyramids?", options: ["Libraries and administrative archives used by pyramid priests", "Causeway temples connecting the pyramid complex to the Nile", "Subsidiary pyramids built for members of the royal family", "Mortuary temples associated with each pyramid"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"these\" in paragraph 2 in the phrase \"The precision with which these blocks were cut and positioned\" refers to _______.", options: ["The stone blocks comprising the Great Pyramid of Khufu", "The cardinal compass points to which the pyramid's sides are aligned", "The construction teams responsible for shaping the pyramid's outer casing", "The surveying instruments used by ancient Egyptian engineers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"sophisticated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Dependent on foreign expertise and technologies imported from other civilisations", "Constructed rapidly under conditions of extreme political pressure", "Unexpectedly small in scale given the size of the construction project", "Highly developed, well-organised, and capable of managing complex operations"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"alignment\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The structural reinforcement used to prevent large stone monuments from shifting over time", "The formal agreement between different research teams about the dating of ancient monuments", "The decorative orientation of carved reliefs on the exterior faces of the pyramids", "The arrangement or positioning of structures in a line or in relation to a specific reference point"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Scholars have long marvelled at how a civilisation without telescopes or written astronomical tables appears to have incorporated precise celestial orientations into structures of such monumental scale.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-065", num: 65, title: "Đề 065", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "The Rise of Hurry Sickness in Modern Society", paragraphs: ["Most people have experienced that uncomfortable feeling of being permanently behind schedule — rushing from one task to the next, eating lunch at a desk, checking emails late at night, and still feeling that there is never enough time. Psychologists have a name for this condition: hurry sickness. First described in the 1970s by cardiologists Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman, hurry sickness is not a medical diagnosis in the formal sense but rather a behavioral pattern in which a person feels a constant and urgent need to do more things in less and less time. Decades after it was first named, hurry sickness appears to be more widespread than ever before.", "The symptoms of hurry sickness are easy to recognize once you know what to look for. People affected by it often feel irritated when forced to wait — whether in a queue at the supermarket, at a traffic light, or on hold during a phone call. They tend to speak quickly, finish other people's sentences, and grow impatient during conversations that do not seem to be moving fast enough. Many sufferers report a constant low-level anxiety that does not disappear even on weekends or holidays, because the feeling of urgency has become so deeply ingrained that the brain struggles to switch off. In some cases, even moments of rest feel wrong — like time being wasted rather than enjoyed.", "Experts point to several factors that have contributed to the rise of hurry sickness in recent decades. The proliferation of digital technology is frequently cited as a primary driver. Smartphones and constant internet connectivity have created an expectation that people should be reachable and responsive at all hours of the day and night. Email inboxes fill up overnight; social media feeds update by the second; news alerts arrive at any moment. The result is a cultural environment in which slowness is increasingly associated with failure or laziness, and speed is treated as a marker of ambition, competence, and success.", "The workplace has played a particularly significant role in normalizing hurry sickness. In many professional environments, being visibly busy has become a status symbol. Employees who work long hours, reply to messages instantly, and claim to have no time for breaks are sometimes admired as dedicated and hardworking, while those who maintain clear boundaries and leave the office on time may be quietly judged as less committed. This culture of busyness makes it difficult for individuals to slow down even when they genuinely want to, because doing so can feel socially or professionally risky.", "The health consequences of living in a state of constant hurry are significant and well documented. Chronic time pressure activates the body's stress response, flooding the bloodstream with hormones such as adrenaline and cortisol. Over time, persistently elevated levels of these stress hormones can contribute to high blood pressure, weakened immunity, and an increased risk of cardiovascular disease. People with hurry sickness also tend to sleep poorly, because the hyperactive mental state associated with the condition makes it hard to wind down at night. Research has also linked chronic busyness with reduced creativity and impaired decision-making, suggesting that the pursuit of productivity can ultimately undermine it.", "Perhaps most paradoxically, hurry sickness can make people less effective at the very tasks they are rushing to complete. When the brain is in a constant state of urgency, it tends to focus on what is immediate rather than what is important, leading to a pattern of reactive behavior in which small and urgent demands crowd out more meaningful long-term goals. Studies in cognitive psychology have shown that people under high time pressure are more likely to make errors, overlook details, and struggle with complex problem-solving. The irony is that slowing down — taking time to plan, reflect, and rest — often produces better outcomes than relentless hurrying ever could.", "There is growing recognition among psychologists and workplace experts that addressing hurry sickness requires changes at both the individual and organizational level. On a personal level, practices such as mindfulness meditation, deliberate scheduling of downtime, and setting firm boundaries around work hours have all shown promise in helping people disengage from the urgency cycle. At the organizational level, companies that have introduced policies such as no-email-after-hours rules, mandatory lunch breaks, and protected time for deep focused work have reported improvements in employee wellbeing and overall productivity. The evidence increasingly suggests that the path to getting more done may, counter-intuitively, involve doing less — but doing it more deliberately and with greater attention."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["People who work long hours are always more productive than those who maintain a healthy work-life balance.", "Hurry sickness is a serious medical condition that requires immediate clinical treatment.", "Hurry sickness is a growing behavioral pattern with wide-ranging causes, consequences, and potential solutions.", "Digital technology is the sole cause of the modern obsession with speed and busyness."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, who first described hurry sickness in the 1970s?", options: ["Workplace consultants advising companies on employee wellbeing", "Two cardiologists named Meyer Friedman and Ray Rosenman", "A team of cognitive psychologists studying workplace stress", "Digital technology researchers studying the effects of smartphones"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is described as a symptom of hurry sickness?", options: ["Speaking very slowly and carefully in order to appear more professional", "A strong preference for working alone rather than in teams", "Feeling completely relaxed and carefree during weekends and holidays", "Becoming irritated when required to wait, even for a short time"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which health problem is linked to persistently high levels of stress hormones caused by chronic time pressure?", options: ["Frequent headaches and problems with digestion", "Increased risk of cardiovascular disease and high blood pressure", "Reduced ability to concentrate during physical exercise", "Loss of appetite and difficulty maintaining a healthy body weight"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the supermarket queue that causes feelings of irritation", "the constant urgency felt during phone calls", "the low-level anxiety that people feel on weekdays", "hurry sickness as a behavioral pattern"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does workplace culture contribute to hurry sickness?", options: ["Managers actively punish employees who take lunch breaks or leave work on time.", "Being visibly busy is treated as a mark of dedication, making it socially difficult to slow down.", "Employees are rewarded financially for replying to messages outside of normal working hours.", "Companies deliberately give workers more tasks than can realistically be completed in a day."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"proliferation\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the careful regulation of something by an authority", "the rapid spread and increase of something across a wide area", "the replacement of something old with a more advanced version", "the gradual disappearance of something once considered important"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"ingrained\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["temporarily present but likely to fade", "recently introduced and unfamiliar", "clearly visible and easy to identify", "deeply fixed and difficult to change"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's purpose in writing this passage?", options: ["To explain what hurry sickness is, why it has spread, how it affects people, and how it might be reduced", "To warn readers that hurry sickness is an unavoidable consequence of living in the modern world", "To persuade readers to give up their smartphones and disconnect completely from digital technology", "To criticize businesses and employers for deliberately causing stress in their workers"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an organizational strategy for reducing hurry sickness?", options: ["Setting aside protected time during the workday for focused, uninterrupted work", "Requiring employees to take a proper lunch break each day", "Offering free mindfulness meditation classes to all members of staff", "Introducing rules that restrict work-related emails outside of office hours"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Vital Role of Bees in Global Ecosystems", paragraphs: ["Few creatures on Earth have a greater impact on the natural world than the humble bee. With more than 20,000 known species distributed across every continent except Antarctica, bees are remarkable not only for their diversity but for the indispensable ecological services they provide. Chief among these is pollination — the transfer of pollen from the male part of one flower to the female part of another, enabling fertilization and the production of seeds and fruit. It is no exaggeration to say that the global food supply, as well as the health of wild plant communities, depends heavily on the continued existence and wellbeing of bee populations worldwide.", "The relationship between bees and flowering plants is one of the most celebrated examples of mutualism in the natural world. As a bee forages for nectar — the sugar-rich liquid that serves as its primary energy source — it inadvertently picks up grains of pollen on the fine hairs that cover its body. When the bee moves to the next flower, some of this pollen is transferred, fertilizing the plant. The plant benefits by reproducing successfully; the bee benefits by obtaining both nectar and pollen, the latter being a critical source of protein used to feed developing larvae. This exchange, refined over tens of millions of years of co-evolution, has shaped the diversity of flowering plant species that we see today.", "The economic value of bee pollination is staggering. Estimates by the Food and Agriculture Organization of the United Nations suggest that bees and other pollinators are directly responsible for sustaining crop production worth between 235 and 577 billion US dollars annually. Crops that are heavily dependent on insect pollination include almonds, blueberries, cherries, cucumbers, and coffee — foods that form a significant part of the human diet in many parts of the world. In regions where wild bee populations have declined sharply, farmers have been forced to resort to expensive and labor-intensive hand pollination or to rent commercially managed honeybee hives — a practice that carries its own risks of disease transmission between colonies.", "Beyond agriculture, bees play a foundational role in sustaining wild ecosystems. Many of the trees, shrubs, and wildflowers that form the structural backbone of forests, meadows, and wetlands are wholly or partially dependent on bee pollination to reproduce. These plants, in turn, provide habitat and food for a vast array of other organisms — from insects and birds to mammals and reptiles. The removal of bees from an ecosystem can therefore set off a cascade of ecological disruption that extends far beyond the immediate loss of individual plant species, ultimately threatening the integrity of entire habitat communities.", "Unfortunately, bee populations worldwide are experiencing steep and sustained declines driven by a combination of interacting pressures. Habitat loss is widely considered the most significant driver: the conversion of wildflower meadows, hedgerows, and woodland margins to intensive agricultural land eliminates the diverse floral resources that many bee species require to survive. Pesticide exposure is another major concern, with a class of chemicals known as neonicotinoids having been shown in numerous studies to impair bees' ability to navigate, forage, and reproduce. The spread of parasites and pathogens — most notably the Varroa mite, which attacks honeybee colonies — has also contributed significantly to population declines, as has the increasing frequency of extreme weather events associated with climate change.", "Efforts to protect and restore bee populations have taken many forms. At the policy level, the European Union imposed restrictions on the outdoor use of three widely used neonicotinoid pesticides in 2018, citing the unacceptable risk they posed to bees and other pollinators. Conservation organizations have promoted the creation of wildflower corridors — continuous strips of flowering habitat that allow bees to move between fragmented patches of suitable land. Individual citizens have also been encouraged to play a role by planting pollinator-friendly gardens, avoiding the use of pesticides in domestic settings, and supporting local beekeepers who maintain healthy and genetically diverse colonies.", "The plight of bees has become something of a cultural and scientific touchstone in recent years, drawing attention to the broader concept of biodiversity loss and its implications for ecosystem services on which humanity depends. Yet awareness alone is insufficient. Sustaining bee populations in the long term will require coordinated action across governments, agricultural industries, conservation bodies, and individual communities — a level of cooperation that reflects the scale and complexity of the challenge. The fate of the bee is, in a very real sense, intertwined with the fate of the natural systems that support all life on Earth."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A scientific comparison of the pollination methods used by different insect species", "The ecological and economic importance of bees, the threats they face, and efforts to protect them", "The life cycle of honeybee colonies and the role of the queen bee in reproduction", "The economic profits that the beekeeping industry generates for farmers around the world"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the estimated annual value of crop production that depends on bee and pollinator activity?", options: ["Between 50 and 100 billion US dollars", "Between 235 and 577 billion US dollars", "More than one trillion US dollars", "Approximately 150 billion US dollars"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following has been shown to impair bees' ability to navigate and reproduce?", options: ["A class of pesticides called neonicotinoids used in agricultural settings", "Extreme cold weather events caused by changing ocean temperatures", "The loss of woodland margins converted to residential housing", "The spread of a parasitic mite known as Varroa across honeybee colonies"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"resort\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["turn to something as a reluctant or last alternative", "benefit greatly from a new and more advanced approach", "decide independently without consulting experts", "gradually move away from a previously effective method"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what action did the European Union take in 2018 regarding certain pesticides?", options: ["It banned the import of crops from countries that had not yet regulated neonicotinoid use.", "It required all farmers to obtain a special license before purchasing neonicotinoid chemicals.", "It funded a program to develop new pesticides that would be safer for bee populations.", "It placed restrictions on the outdoor use of three neonicotinoid pesticides to protect pollinators."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is the loss of bees harmful to wild ecosystems beyond the direct effect on plants?", options: ["It eliminates the food source that many species of fish depend on during the summer months.", "It triggers a chain of ecological disruption that ultimately threatens the habitat of many other organisms.", "It causes rivers and wetlands to dry up because fewer plants release moisture into the atmosphere.", "It reduces the amount of oxygen produced by forests, affecting air quality across entire continents."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"inadvertently\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["after a long period of gradual learning", "with great care and deliberate effort", "in a highly efficient and organized manner", "without intending to do so"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"we\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["bees that have evolved alongside flowering plants over millions of years", "people in general as observers of the natural world today", "scientists who study the co-evolution of insects and plants", "farmers who rely on bee pollination for their crop yields"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a cause of declining bee populations?", options: ["The destruction of wildflower habitats through agricultural expansion", "The spread of parasites such as the Varroa mite among honeybee colonies", "The damaging effects of certain pesticides on bees' navigation and reproductive ability", "The introduction of non-native bee species that compete with local populations for resources"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the situation facing bee populations today?", options: ["Indifferent, treating the issue as a minor ecological problem with little relevance to human life", "Dismissive of scientific research, preferring to focus on individual lifestyle choices as the only real solution", "Optimistic, believing that existing conservation efforts are already sufficient to reverse the decline", "Alarmed but constructive, recognizing the severity of the threat while emphasizing the need for coordinated action"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "The Paradox of Social Media Connectivity", paragraphs: ["In the space of roughly two decades, social media platforms have transformed from experimental curiosities into the dominant infrastructure of human social life. Billions of people now use platforms such as Facebook, Instagram, TikTok, and X — formerly Twitter — to communicate with friends and family, discover news, build professional networks, and participate in communities organized around shared interests. The promise at the heart of this transformation was seductive: technology would dissolve the barriers of geography and circumstance, allowing people everywhere to connect with one another more easily and more deeply than ever before. Yet a growing body of psychological and sociological research suggests that this promise has been only partially fulfilled — and that, for many users, the experience of social media connectivity coexists uneasily with persistent feelings of loneliness, inadequacy, and social anxiety.", "The concept that explains much of this tension is sometimes called the comparison trap. Social media platforms are, by their architecture, engines of curated self-presentation. Users systematically select which moments of their lives to share, gravitating toward experiences that appear exciting, successful, or enviable, and editing out the mundane difficulties and private disappointments that constitute the texture of ordinary life. The result is an environment in which every scroll through a feed confronts the viewer with a stream of apparently exceptional lives — exotic holidays, career milestones, social gatherings, and expressions of domestic happiness — against which their own life inevitably appears comparatively dim. Research in social psychology has consistently found that upward social comparison of this kind is strongly associated with reduced self-esteem and elevated depressive symptoms, particularly among adolescents and young adults.", "The paradox deepens when one examines the relationship between social media use and loneliness. [1] It might seem reasonable to suppose that a technology explicitly designed to facilitate connection would reduce feelings of isolation — and for some users, particularly those who are geographically or physically isolated, this does appear to be the case. [2] However, multiple large-scale longitudinal studies have found that heavier social media use is, on average, associated with greater — not lesser — loneliness over time. [3] Researchers have proposed several explanations for this counterintuitive finding. One is that the shallow, asynchronous interactions typical of social media may displace the deeper, face-to-face engagements that are most effective at satisfying human social needs. Another is that the passive consumption of others' content — scrolling without actively interacting — has been shown to be particularly corrosive to wellbeing, producing a sense of social exclusion rather than social belonging. [4]", "Attention has also turned to the design choices that govern how social media platforms operate. Critics argue that these platforms are engineered not primarily to promote human connection but to maximize what the industry calls \"engagement\" — the total amount of time users spend on the platform and the frequency with which they return. Features such as the infinite scroll, variable-reward notification systems, and algorithmically curated feeds are all, in this analysis, tools of behavioral conditioning that exploit well-documented vulnerabilities in human psychology to keep users returning compulsively, regardless of whether the experience leaves them feeling better or worse. Former employees of major technology companies have testified publicly that the platforms are deliberately designed to be as difficult to leave as possible — a claim that has added fuel to calls for stronger regulatory oversight.", "The effects of social media are not, however, uniformly negative, and a simplistic condemnation of the technology risks overlooking the genuine benefits it provides. For marginalized individuals — those belonging to minority communities, people with disabilities, or those living in environments where their identities are stigmatized — social media can provide access to communities of support and understanding that would otherwise be entirely unavailable. Research conducted during the COVID-19 pandemic found that social media played a meaningful role in sustaining social bonds for many people during periods of enforced physical separation. Creative professionals, activists, and small business owners have also found social media to be a powerful and democratizing tool for reaching audiences and building movements that would previously have required access to expensive and gatekept distribution channels.", "The challenge for researchers, policymakers, and platform designers is to understand what distinguishes the uses of social media that promote wellbeing from those that undermine it. The emerging consensus points to active versus passive use as a particularly significant variable. Users who engage with social media primarily by creating content, communicating directly with specific individuals, and participating in focused community discussions tend to report more positive outcomes than those who primarily consume content passively. The frequency and duration of use also matter, with research suggesting that moderate engagement is associated with better outcomes than either complete abstinence or heavy, habitual use.", "As awareness of social media's psychological costs has grown, so too have calls for reform at the platform level. Proposals include the introduction of transparent algorithmic controls that allow users to understand and adjust how content is selected for them, the removal of publicly visible engagement metrics such as \"like\" counts that have been identified as particularly damaging to adolescent self-image, and mandatory age verification systems to limit the exposure of young users to content known to carry psychological risks. Several countries have moved to introduce legislation governing social media platforms' obligations toward their users, and the broader debate about how to balance innovation with protection has become one of the defining regulatory conversations of the digital age."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Despite offering genuine benefits, social media connectivity often produces psychological costs such as loneliness and reduced self-esteem, and the challenge lies in understanding how to distinguish its harmful uses from its helpful ones.", "Social media companies have successfully resisted all attempts at government regulation, making reform impossible in the near future.", "The primary cause of rising mental health problems among young people today is the amount of time they spend on social media platforms.", "Social media platforms are deliberately harmful and should be banned for users under the age of eighteen."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why does the \"comparison trap\" tend to lower users' self-esteem?", options: ["Platform algorithms deliberately show users content from people who are wealthier and more successful than they are.", "Social media notifications interrupt users' work and reduce the sense of achievement they feel at the end of the day.", "Users encounter a curated stream of apparently exceptional lives that makes their own lives seem less satisfying by comparison.", "Users are frequently exposed to professionally produced advertisements that promote unrealistic standards of beauty."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did large-scale longitudinal studies find about the relationship between social media use and loneliness?", options: ["Loneliness was found to be caused primarily by the quality of content on social media rather than by how long users spent on it.", "The relationship between social media and loneliness varied too greatly across age groups to draw any general conclusions.", "Heavy social media users reported significantly lower levels of loneliness than those who rarely used the platforms.", "On average, greater social media use was associated with increased rather than decreased loneliness over time."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the people who share exciting and successful moments online", "the researchers studying upward social comparison", "the social media platforms generating curated content", "the users who are viewing other people's posts in their feed"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, for which group of people has social media been found to provide genuine support?", options: ["Retired people who use social media to stay informed about current news and local events", "Professional athletes who use social media to build their personal brand and reach sponsors", "Marginalized individuals who would otherwise have no access to communities of understanding and support", "School students who use social media primarily to complete group assignments with classmates"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes social media users who tend to report more positive wellbeing outcomes?", options: ["They use social media primarily on desktop computers rather than on mobile phones.", "They follow a larger and more diverse range of accounts, exposing themselves to a wider variety of viewpoints.", "They engage actively by creating content and communicating directly, rather than passively consuming others' content.", "They spend fewer total hours on social media platforms than the average user."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"displace\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["combine with something to create a new result", "improve significantly over a period of time", "measure the quality of something against a standard", "take the place of something and push it aside"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"democratizing\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["organizing something according to a system based on majority decision-making", "making something politically neutral so that it appeals to a wider audience", "restricting access to something so that only qualified users may benefit from it", "making something that was previously exclusive more widely available and accessible"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a proposed platform-level reform to reduce the psychological harms of social media?", options: ["Introducing systems to verify the age of users before they can access certain content", "Giving users transparent controls over the algorithms that determine which content they see", "Removing publicly visible metrics such as the number of likes on a post", "Requiring platforms to pay financial compensation to users who experience mental health harm"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This finding challenges the intuitive assumption that more connection must mean less isolation.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Escalating Threat of Marine Heatwaves", paragraphs: ["The ocean has long been regarded as a stabilizing force in the Earth's climate system — a vast thermal reservoir whose enormous heat capacity buffers the planet against the more rapid temperature fluctuations that characterize the atmosphere above it. [1] This conception, while not inaccurate, has increasingly been complicated by the emergence of marine heatwaves: discrete periods of anomalously high sea surface temperatures that persist for days, weeks, or months over spatially coherent regions of the ocean. [2] Far from passive recipients of atmospheric warming, the ocean's thermal dynamics have begun to generate their own destabilizing events — ones that, according to the scientific literature published over the past two decades, are growing in frequency, intensity, and duration at a rate that has outpaced even the more alarming projections of earlier climate models. [3] The implications of this acceleration extend well beyond the ocean itself, touching fisheries, coastal economies, atmospheric circulation patterns, and the ecological integrity of some of the most biologically diverse systems on Earth. [4]", "Marine heatwaves are defined not in absolute temperature terms but relative to local historical baselines. An event qualifies as a marine heatwave when sea surface temperatures in a given region exceed the 90th percentile of the climatological average for that location for a period of at least five consecutive days. This threshold-based definition means that a marine heatwave in the frigid waters of the sub-Antarctic carries entirely different absolute temperatures from one occurring in the tropical Indo-Pacific, yet both are equally anomalous relative to what organisms in those regions have adapted to tolerate. The biological consequences of a marine heatwave are therefore not simply a function of the temperatures reached but of the degree to which those temperatures deviate from the local norm to which resident species are accustomed.", "The mechanisms through which marine heatwaves develop are varied and often involve the interaction of multiple atmospheric and oceanic processes. Persistent high-pressure atmospheric systems can suppress wind-driven surface mixing, allowing the uppermost layer of the ocean to accumulate heat without the dissipation that normal turbulence would provide. Anomalies in large-scale ocean circulation — such as weakening of the upwelling systems that ordinarily transport cold, nutrient-rich water from depth to the surface — can similarly create conditions favorable to surface warming. In some cases, teleconnections between distant climate systems, such as those mediated by El Niño-Southern Oscillation events, impose additional thermal forcing on already warm baseline conditions, compounding the intensity of events that would otherwise remain within less ecologically damaging bounds.", "The ecological impacts of marine heatwaves are both severe and extensively documented. Coral reefs — among the most biodiverse ecosystems on the planet — are particularly vulnerable to even modest temperature anomalies. When seawater temperatures exceed corals' thermal tolerance thresholds by as little as one degree Celsius for a sustained period, corals expel the symbiotic algae, known as zooxanthellae, that inhabit their tissues and provide them with the majority of their nutritional needs through photosynthesis. This process, termed coral bleaching, leaves the coral structurally intact but physiologically stressed and, if thermal stress persists, can lead to mortality within weeks. The 2016 and 2017 bleaching events associated with a sustained marine heatwave across the Great Barrier Reef resulted in mortality across more than fifty percent of the reef's shallow-water corals — a loss of biological complexity that researchers have characterized as effectively irreversible on human timescales.", "Beyond coral systems, marine heatwaves disrupt the productivity of the ocean food web at its foundational level by suppressing phytoplankton growth. Phytoplankton — microscopic photosynthetic organisms that form the base of nearly all marine food chains — require cool, nutrient-rich water to thrive. When marine heatwaves stratify the water column and cut off the supply of deep, nutrient-laden water to the surface, phytoplankton blooms collapse, triggering cascading effects through zooplankton, forage fish, and the larger predators — including commercially important species and marine mammals — that depend on them. The 2019–2020 marine heatwave in the northeast Pacific, referred to informally as \"the Blob,\" was associated with catastrophic seabird and marine mammal mortality events that drew widespread scientific and public attention to the ecosystem-level consequences of prolonged thermal anomalies.", "The attribution of individual marine heatwave events to anthropogenic climate change requires careful statistical analysis, but the broader trend is now scientifically unambiguous: ocean warming driven by rising concentrations of greenhouse gases has raised the baseline temperatures against which anomalies are measured, meaning that events that would have been statistically rare a century ago now occur with far greater regularity. Research published in leading journals has estimated that marine heatwaves are currently occurring at roughly double the frequency observed in the pre-industrial era, and that warming trajectories consistent with high greenhouse gas emission scenarios could produce conditions in which some ocean regions experience near-permanent marine heatwave states by the end of the twenty-first century.", "Responses to the marine heatwave threat have thus far been characterized more by scientific documentation than by effective mitigation, reflecting both the global scale of the underlying driver and the political difficulty of translating research findings into coordinated action. Monitoring networks have expanded considerably, and early warning systems capable of forecasting marine heatwave events several weeks in advance have been developed, providing fisheries managers and conservation agencies with a modest but meaningful lead time to implement protective measures. Yet scientists and policymakers broadly agree that these adaptive responses, however valuable, cannot substitute for the fundamental emissions reductions that would be required to meaningfully alter the long-term trajectory of ocean warming — a conclusion that lends a note of urgency to international climate negotiations that has thus far produced responses widely regarded as insufficient."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central concern of the passage?", options: ["The growing threat posed by marine heatwaves to ocean ecosystems, food systems, and the climate, and the inadequacy of current responses", "A technical comparison of the different atmospheric mechanisms that cause sea surface temperatures to rise above normal levels", "The discovery that coral bleaching is caused primarily by water pollution rather than by elevated ocean temperatures", "The failure of international climate negotiations to reach binding agreements on greenhouse gas emissions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what conditions must be met for an event to be classified as a marine heatwave?", options: ["Sea surface temperatures must rise above 30 degrees Celsius and remain there for at least two weeks.", "Surface temperatures must remain elevated for a full calendar month across an area larger than one million square kilometers.", "Temperatures must exceed the local 90th percentile historical average for a minimum of five consecutive days.", "The affected ocean region must experience a temperature increase of at least three degrees above its annual mean."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how do persistent high-pressure atmospheric systems contribute to the development of marine heatwaves?", options: ["They increase the rate at which greenhouse gases are absorbed by the ocean surface.", "They draw cold water from deeper ocean layers up to the surface, replacing warmer surface water.", "They reduce wind-driven surface mixing, allowing heat to build up in the uppermost ocean layer.", "They redirect warm tropical ocean currents toward polar regions, raising baseline temperatures globally."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The phrase \"those regions\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the deep-ocean regions where cold, nutrient-rich water originates before rising to the surface", "the coastal areas where marine heatwaves have the most damaging economic impact on fishing communities", "the areas of the ocean where historical temperature records are most complete and reliable", "the frigid sub-Antarctic and tropical Indo-Pacific ocean zones cited as examples of differing absolute temperatures"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Yet the ocean is no longer simply absorbing that warmth — it is increasingly generating its own climate extremes.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what ecological event was associated with the 2019–2020 marine heatwave in the northeast Pacific informally called \"the Blob\"?", options: ["A collapse in phytoplankton populations that triggered mass die-offs of seabirds and marine mammals", "A dramatic reduction in the populations of commercially harvested fish species including salmon and tuna", "The widespread bleaching and subsequent mortality of coral reefs across more than half of the affected region", "The permanent disruption of upwelling systems that had previously sustained one of the world's most productive fisheries"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"dissipation\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the measurement of temperature differences between ocean layers", "the process by which heat is scattered and released into the surroundings", "the transfer of nutrients from deep water to the ocean surface", "the gradual buildup of thermal energy within a confined layer"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"compounding\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["making an existing problem more severe by adding further pressure", "measuring or recording accurately for scientific purposes", "distributing the effects of something more evenly across a wider area", "reversing or canceling out an existing condition"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a response that has been implemented to address the threat of marine heatwaves?", options: ["The development of early warning systems that can forecast marine heatwave events weeks in advance", "The expansion of ocean monitoring networks to track sea surface temperature anomalies", "The use of lead-time forecasts to help fisheries managers take protective action before events occur", "International agreements that set binding limits on the greenhouse gas emissions that drive ocean warming"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the author's view of current international efforts to address ocean warming?", options: ["The author is cautiously optimistic that recent international agreements will produce meaningful reductions in ocean temperatures within a decade.", "The author argues that the problem of ocean warming is too complex to be addressed through international cooperation and should instead focus on local solutions.", "The author suggests that current international responses have fallen short of what the scientific evidence indicates is necessary.", "The author believes that monitoring and early warning systems are sufficient to prevent the worst ecological consequences of marine heatwaves."], answer: 2 }] },
  ] },
];
